ry about him but  the beauty of what he does because i  actually think he's brilliant on how he  what he puts on the screen is that on  one level you are saying  wow this is amazing and on the other  you're cringing you're squirming you're  cringing and then the next minute you're  doubled over in laughter yeah  you're on a roller coaster but he's  always making a point yes he got a lot  of attention when he spoke out about  social media  oh yeah 2019 where people thought let me  take a look at this  because he made some really very  powerful and i mentioned he went after  that abby hoffman part  way back in 2007 he actually called  stephen spielberg  on the phone and said i want this part  and he said well it's got a very  distinct accent you don't have  and he said i'll give you two weeks to  learn it and his criticism  and what happened well it waited 13  years for them to actually make the film  he interrupted borat  the filming of it to go do the chicago  seven movies that he wanted  so badly yeah his criticism of social  media may be his  more lasting legacy however and it has  not ended with that speech that's right  on election day  with donald trump leaving the oval  office he had a picture of donald trump  and  uh mark zuckerberg and he said one down  one to go  he really thinks that social media has  become as he put it a sewer of bigotry  and they need to take responsibility for  what's on their platform we're beginning  to see that  i was gonna say that could be his legacy  because certainly moving in that  direction yeah  they would say they've been doing it all  along but they've definitely stepped it  up  he also wrote a piece for i believe it  was time magazine about it as well yeah  so he continues to be vocal about that  anthony that was a really good piece  he's a very interesting guy really  really good  he's a very interesting guy

